## This template

The whole demo runs off **USART2** (PA2/PA3 @ 115200) plus one LED on **PD12**.
No sensors — the point is the concurrency layer, not the peripherals.

### Pinout

| Function | Pin | Notes |
|----------|-----|-------|
| USART2 TX | PA2 | AF7, 115200 8N1 |
| USART2 RX | PA3 | AF7 |
| LED | PD12 | toggled by the Consumer on each event |

### What `main.cpp` does

This template is built on the SDK **concurrency layer** (`STM32_USE_SYSTEM` +
FreeRTOS: `import system.work_queue; import system.executor; import
system.signal_bus;`). Two components communicate only through a typed channel:

- `Producer` — a component with its **own FreeRTOS task**. Its `run()` loop
  publishes a `HeartbeatEvent{seq, value}` on the channel every 500 ms. It has
  no reference to the Consumer.
- `Consumer<Uart, GpioPin>` — a **reactive** component with **no task of its
  own**. In `onBind()` it subscribes its `onBeat()` handler to the channel;
  every handler call runs on the executor task. It has no reference to the
  Producer.

Flow:

1. `__initialize_hardware()` enables GPIOA, GPIOD and USART2 clocks.
2. `struct SignalDemo` is the composition root: the UART, LED, the
   `SingleThreadExecutor`, the `Channel<HeartbeatEvent, 4>` (injected with the
   executor), then the two components — each wired by reference to earlier
   members.
3. `app.boot()` runs `system::bootstrap(producer, consumer)`: the four phases
   `Register → Init → Bind → Start`. `Consumer::onBind()` is where the
   subscription happens; a full subscriber table (more than `MaxSubs`) would
   surface as a non-`Ok` `BootReport`.
4. `main` prints the `BootReport`, runs a one-shot `workQueueDispatchSelfCheck()`
   (RTOS-free, proves FIFO/priority ordering before the scheduler starts),
   creates the Producer task and starts the scheduler.

The `SingleThreadExecutor` owns its own task (created at static init, runs after
`startScheduler()`); publishing a value posts an internal `WorkItem` to it, so
all `onBeat()` fan-out is serialized on that one task — no mutex in the handler.

### Expected behaviour

- On boot USART2 prints `=== signal-bus-demo bootstrap ===`, then
  `all components started` and `workqueue self-check: PASS`.
- Every 500 ms a new line appears: `beat #<n> value=<±v>` with an increasing
  `n` and a value sweeping a sawtooth between -100 and +99.
- The PD12 LED toggles once per event (≈1 Hz visible blink).

### How to verify

1. Power the board, open the serial port at 115200 8N1.
2. Confirm the bootstrap banner and `self-check: PASS`.
3. Watch the `beat #...` lines arrive once per 500 ms with monotonically
   increasing `seq`, and the LED toggling in lockstep.

### When to extend

- Add a second subscriber (`MaxSubs` is 4): e.g. a component that accumulates a
  running average of `value` and publishes its own derived event on another
  channel — subscribers never see each other.
- Move heavy work out of `onBeat()` by posting a separate `WorkItem` to
  `exec` — keep signal handlers short (they run on the shared executor task).
- Replace the periodic Producer loop with an ISR that calls
  `exec.postFromISR(...)` to defer processing out of interrupt context.
